ci(172,statement='For A^(p,i)=Γ^(p−i)(Φ)⊗Λ^i(Ψ), the only homotopy occurs when p=i=ℓ, with value k; the components with i<p are flasque.',status_on_pages='derived from the preceding homotopy lemmas',**{'class':'B'},fate='Cartan (1976), §7, pp. 269–271, publishes the standard model and its homotopy calculation, explicitly attributing the construction to Grothendieck’s lecture of 12 December 1975. This is class B because the publication is Cartan’s, while its authorial attribution is preserved.',computation='Uses the explicit simplicial contraction (C166), exact exterior normalization (C168), and Eilenberg–Zilber shift (C170); see mathematical_checks.md.')
ci(173,statement='Weight-p forms compute τ≤p RΓ(X,k): their cohomology is H^i(X,k) for i≤p and zero for i>p.',status_on_pages='proof sketched using the cycle object in good truncation',**{'class':'B'},fate='Cartan (1976), §7, pp. 269–271, gives this weighted comparison over Z and its binomial-scaled products, explicitly crediting Grothendieck’s 12 December 1975 lecture. It is an additive weightwise theorem, with a specified product factor, not an ordinary unweighted integral ring equivalence.',computation='The top degree i=p is retained by replacing the last term with Z^p. The proof in mathematical_checks.md does not infer this inclusive boundary from a strict inequality.')
ci(174,statement='The coefficient ring is to be completed for the filtration generated by the divided-power tails; a completed model is being set up.',status_on_pages='setup begins; no finished completion theorem on this leaf',fate='Scan 233 defines the filtration and completeness setup. Its continuation in the supplied 235–281 packet is assigned to the late-package agent. No limit-exactness or completed-model theorem is inferred from the heading.')
ci(175,statement='Under the donor’s henselian and semistable setup, maps from the indicated fixed Barsotti–Tate sources factor through the fixed part; toric and abelian quotients have the stated dual Tate-module descriptions.',status_on_pages='donor theorems, with an explicit Tate proviso where used',fate='Direct publication match in SGA7 IX 5.5–5.9. De Jong (1998), Corollary 1.2, later removes the characteristic-zero restriction from the generic-fibre full-faithfulness ingredient for p-divisible groups over a DVR; it does not erase every other hypothesis of the fixed-part construction.')
ci(176,statement='The abelian parts of the special fibres of dual abelian varieties are canonically dual; the displayed isogeny is in fact the required perfect duality.',status_on_pages='general proof deferred to §7.4.6',fate='Published in SGA7 IX 5.4 with the promised biextension argument in §7.4.6. This belongs to the earlier donor stratum and is not an unanswered question of the PD lecture.')
ci(177,statement='The fixed-part and toric orthogonality assertions, with the induced abelian-part pairing, hold for all primes including the residue characteristic.',status_on_pages='partial reduction on these leaves; general proof deferred',fate='Published SGA7 IX 5.2.6 and 5.3, with the biextension analysis in §7.4.6–7.4.7. The draft’s stated Tate proviso must still be recorded where its argument uses it; de Jong updates that separate input.')
ci(178,statement='For the pro-ℓ Barsotti–Tate morphism over a connected Noetherian base in Lemma 5.2.5, vanishing, being an isogeny, or being an isomorphism at the specified fibre implies the corresponding global property.',status_on_pages='proof called easy and left to the reader',fate='SGA7 IX 5.2.5 publishes the same lemma with the proof left to the reader; §7.4.7 treats the needed applications. This run collated the exact assertion and hypotheses; it did not independently re-prove a general BT rigidity theorem.')
ci(179,statement='An abelian variety over the fraction field of the donor’s henselian trait has good reduction exactly when its indicated Barsotti–Tate group extends; the p-primary statement is made with a characteristic-zero proviso.',status_on_pages='an older conjecture is now stated as a corollary; proof is partial on the isolated leaf',fate='SGA7 IX 5.10 publishes the criterion with its Tate proviso. De Jong (1998), §2 Criterion 2.5, gives good and semistable reduction equivalence in characteristic p. His 1999 erratum replaces an incorrect automatic kernel/cokernel assertion by an extra condition in Definition 2.2 of semistable reduction; it explicitly leaves the rest unchanged. Theorem 2.6 is a different endomorphism theorem and is not the locator for this criterion.')
ci(180,statement='The formal torus and fixed-part construction algebraizes under completeness, and the completeness assumption can subsequently be removed as stated in §6.1.',status_on_pages='formal construction with a deferred removal of a hypothesis',fate='Published SGA7 IX 5.1 invokes SGA3 IX 3.6 bis and EGA III 4.8 for the formal construction, and §6.1 treats the completeness issue. Those citations are part of the donor proof; their use here is source comparison rather than an independent reconstruction of formal algebraization.')
ci(181,statement='A finite-order automorphism of the stated semiabelian variety that fixes n-torsion is the identity for n≥3 and has order at most 2 for n=2. A group all of whose elements square to 1 is commutative.',status_on_pages='Serre lemma and continuation of its proof',fate='Published SGA7 IX 4.7 contains the torsion-congruence argument. The transcribed v/u generator and n/m exponent are flagged without correction. The exponent-two group assertion follows directly from (ab)^(−1)=b^(−1)a^(−1).',computation='If a²=b²=(ab)²=1, then ab=(ab)^(−1)=ba. This is a direct proof, with no finite enumeration used.')
ci(182,statement='The Raynaud extension supplies a functorial rigid-analytic exact sequence 0→M^an→G^an→A^an→0, compatible with base change; the map e to A is uniquely characterized by its formal-completion restriction.',status_on_pages='existence referred to Raynaud; uniqueness argued',fate='Published SGA7 IX 14.1.7–14.1.12, printed pp. 507–509. Remark 14.1.12(a) explicitly identifies existence as nontrivial and refers to Raynaud; uniqueness follows from connectedness. Later uniformization literature develops this construction, but no independent existence proof is claimed here.')
ci(183,statement='A purely algebraic-geometric description of the lattice embedding i:M→G is desired.',status_on_pages='explicitly unknown to the writer; an arithmetic characterization is promised in §14.4',fate='The same lack of a purely algebraic-geometric description remains in published SGA7 IX 14.1.12(a), printed p. 509. Later Raynaud–Mumford degeneration data and 1-motives are relevant, but this run did not verify an exact canonical answer to that wording. It is not declared an open problem as of 2026.')
ci(184,statement='The dual lattice embedding obtained from the Poincaré trivialization should agree with the embedding for the dual abelian variety, possibly with the opposite sign.',status_on_pages='explicit sign doubt',fate='The opposite-sign question also survives in published SGA7 IX 14.1.12(b), printed p. 509. A comparison of Raynaud and Grothendieck monodromy pairings under fixed conventions does not by itself settle the sign of this precise embedding formula.')
ci(185,statement='The extension data M→B and trivializations of the pulled-back Poincaré biextension correspond canonically to lifts M→G, and dually.',status_on_pages='formal correspondence asserted',fate='Published SGA7 IX 14.1.4–14.1.6, using VIII 3.7. This is a biextension universal property in the donor text, separate from the free PD-algebra universal property.')
ci(186,statement='The torsion exact sequences of rigid uniformization give 0→TℓG→TℓA→M⊗Zℓ→0, and the maps coincide with the algebraically constructed maps.',status_on_pages='asserted with a proof continuing beyond the supplied leaf',fate='Published SGA7 IX 14.2.1–14.2.3, with the proof continuing after printed p. 510. Coleman (2000), §2, is a bibliographical lead for equality of the associated Raynaud and Grothendieck monodromy pairings; the source remained inaccessible here, so it is not treated as a checked proof of every map or sign.')
ci(187,statement='For a flat finite-type morphism of varieties over an algebraically closed field, the locus of points Gorenstein in their fibre is open. If the morphism is proper, the base locus of entirely Gorenstein fibres is open.',status_on_pages='proof explicitly attributed to M. Stoia; its beginning and end are supplied',fate='Stacks tag 0C09, Lemma 48.25.11, proves openness for flat morphisms locally of finite presentation between schemes. Properness sends the closed complement to a closed set and gives the base statement. Bănică–Stoia (June 1979), bibliography [6], identifies Stoia’s same-title C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris 281 paper of November 1975; that original paper was not obtained. The 1979 analytic analogue is a separate verified theorem.')

